+# FAA Eastern Region shipments and Newark's local-control clock entry
+
+November 9, 2026 Eastern. B129 / PD07. Two Commission release members preserve Eastern Region shipment covers and a separate Newark chronology. A related Newark daily log records a local-control time-stamp problem and manual hour adjustment. [Commission collection and arrangement](https://www.archives.gov/research/9-11/commission-series.html) · [Exact sources and field checks](SOURCE-PINS.json)
+
+## Regional shipment covers and their stated limits
+
+- **October 8, 2003.** The Eastern Region certified index accompanies a shipment of copies and describes materials retained by the region's Air Traffic Division Quality Assurance staff and its branch offices. **October 9** is the date of a separate cover in the packet. A following page lists Regional Operations Center documents. [Team 8 Box 15 item 697, pp. 1–2, 4–6](https://archive.org/download/911datasets/NARA_9-11_Commission_Records_Released_Jan_14_2009.zip)
+- **October 10, 2003.** The region describes a subsequent shipment of recently identified responsive records. It says that email attachments “have not been printed and shipped” and that they are available on request. This documents an exclusion from the shipment described in that cover. Whether particular attachments were later requested or delivered remains open. The copy of the packet has no embedded files. [Item 697, p. 3](https://archive.org/download/911datasets/NARA_9-11_Commission_Records_Released_Jan_14_2009.zip)
+
+Newark's October 20 index separately describes an October 8 FedEx shipment and says item 12 was omitted from the original package. The shared October 8 date does not identify a shared package. No reviewed airbill, receipt or itemized intake joins the regional covers to Newark item 12. The four Newark airbill entries, EWR-24–26 and EWR-30, and the EWR-31 tape transfer remain inventory leads. The finding aid has no catalogue link on those rows. [Newark index and log packet](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/5efb3b36e4b27bdbd84341e8fa7b0667bec574d7/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-commission-aea-tower-certification-2026-11-09/README.md) · [FAA finding aid, Box 9](https://www.archives.gov/research/foia/faa-finding-aid#:~:text=EWR%2D24)
+
+## Newark time-stamp and ordering observations
+
+Form 7230-4 in item 698, p. 3, shows the entry label **E1005**. The entry reports a possible problem with the **LC time stamp**, says it did not advance to the next hour, and records that tower personnel manually advanced the hour setting. The label and equipment description are the observations. Which device or recording channel it affected, the duration of the error, and whether the form's times share that clock remain open. No correction was applied to any recorded time. [Item 698, pp. 3–4](https://archive.org/download/911datasets/NARA_9-11_Commission_Records_Released_Jan_14_2009.zip)
+
+| Newark tower chronology, Team 8 Box 17, p. 1 | Newark daily log, Team 8 Box 15 item 698, p. 4 |
+| --- | --- |
+| Describes the supervisor's access-road request, then lists a south-departure stop and a stop of all departures in that order on the page. | **1259** stop White. **1300** stop Lanna and Biggy. **1304** stop departures. **1306** request to shut off the tower road. |
+| Places the airport closure “Approximately 10 minutes later” after the all-departure-stop sentence. | **1307** airport closed. **1329** Port Authority advises the airport will remain closed. |
+
+The printed log labels place the departure-stop rows before the road request. The chronology presents the road request first. The chronology's approximate interval, the three-minute difference between the log's **1304** and **1307** labels, and the later **1329** advisory remain separate. The three minutes are arithmetic on displayed labels. The order of narration, equivalence of specific events, time basis and elapsed interval remain unresolved. There is no established causal link between these observations and the LC clock entry. [Exact rows and page references](SOURCE-PINS.json)
+
+## Scope and next records
+
+Seven pages in the two new PDFs were screened using their text layers. Selected shipment language, sequence and log rows were checked in targeted renders, including the reused item 698 pages. The byte-range requests matched the ZIP index, CRC and SHA-256 for both new members. Neither PDF has embedded files. The Newark chronology corresponds in title and subject to an indexed Newark chronology, but its identity with FAA EWR-9 or any of the five listed copies is unverified.
+
+The next discriminating records are the Newark airbills and itemized intake, the regional electronic-mail attachment manifest and onward receipt, and the Newark LC clock setting or maintenance record with the original timestamped recordings and independent matching events. PD01–PD04 still require the actual corrected May 2003 answer and receipt, signed administrative disposition, and recommendation-specific response and closure records.
